# Every variable this project has. All four are PUBLIC values, and that is the
# only kind this file may ever hold.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* is not a secret store. Anything with that prefix is substituted
# into the JavaScript bundle at build time and ships inside the APK, where
# `unzip -p app.apk assets/index.android.bundle | strings | grep` will find it in
# a few seconds. A base URL is public by nature — every request the app makes
# announces it — so it is the right kind of value for this mechanism.
#
# Cloudinary, Pusher, Anthropic, the database URL and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET stay
# on the Next.js server and are reached through server routes. None of them
# belongs in this file, in app.json's `extra`, or anywhere else in this project.
#
# The API base URL below is a DEFAULT, not a setting: it is compiled in, and the
# gear on the sign-in screen overrides it at runtime and stores the override in
# SecureStore. Changing this file still needs `npx expo start --clear`; changing
# it from the gear does not.
#
# NOT localhost. On a phone or an emulator, localhost is the device itself.
#   Real device on the same Wi-Fi:  http://<your-machine-LAN-IP>:3000
#   Android emulator:               http://10.0.2.2:3000
#   iOS simulator:                  http://localhost:3000
#
# Do not fill this in by hand for a physical device. `npm run phone` works out
# the right address, tells you when this line disagrees with it, and will write
# the correction for you with -WriteEnv. See "Reaching the dev server from a
# phone" in README.md.
#
# Start Next.js with `next dev -H 0.0.0.0` so it accepts connections that did
# not come from the machine it is running on.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.10:3000

# ── Google sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OAuth CLIENT IDS, not secrets. A client id is public by definition — it is in
# the URL of every consent screen Google shows — and an installed app is a
# PUBLIC CLIENT: there is nowhere in an APK to keep a secret, which is why the
# native flow uses PKCE instead of a client secret. Do NOT put
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET in this file; it belongs to the web app and stays on the
# Next.js server.
#
# The one the app uses is chosen by platform: Android builds send the Android
# client's id, iOS builds the iOS one. Whichever is used becomes the `aud` claim
# on the ID token, and the backend only accepts audiences listed in its own
# GOOGLE_NATIVE_CLIENT_IDS — so both ends have to be filled in or the exchange
# 401s. See README.md § Google sign-in for the console steps.
#
# Leave these blank and the app still builds and password sign-in still works;
